					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=617958&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609233419" alt="Communism will win" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=617958)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;" >Communism will win</a></span> <span class="odate time_1366872596 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Apr 2013 06:49</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1760016">
									

<p>Smapti, that's the equivalent that Dmatix could not write <a href="/quiet-days">Quiet Days</a> because he did not create the website. The wiki is a collaborative project, and canon is fluid. Your argument is silly.</p>
<p>And, if you want, look at it this way: It's entirely possible to write a spiritual ending to something and continue to write things after it<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-136752-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-136752-1')">1</a></sup>. Word of God has it that the Spongebob Squarepants movie is the end of the series despite the fact that they continue to churn out episodes (to the best of my knowledge). Unless one chooses to mechanically read each SPC story in order of creation date, there is no reason that this cannot describe an ending to the Shark Punching Center.</p>
<p>I'm not telling you to like it, but you don't need to come up with bizarre explanations every time something just doesn't grab you.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-136752-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-136752-1')">1</a>. I personally feel like the SPC has been run into the ground, and that none need to be written after this, but I don't care if you don't.</div>
</div>

